# {title}

Everything you see in Quote Slicer lives under one root component, `+page.svelte`, and its centerpiece, `QuoteWorkbench.svelte`. Think of this article as the map: what each piece is responsible for, and how they are wired together. The *behavior* inside each piece — how the Link tool works, how the Line tool works, how the tools animate into one another — is covered in its own article and linked from here.

## Why one root, one workbench

The four [[overview#the-four-tools|tools]] are not four separate screens. They are four ways of looking at the *same* source text, target text, and token data. So rather than swapping whole pages in and out, the app is built as **one persistent component tree** that changes which parts are interactive and how things look. That single decision is what lets the [[tools-and-mediums/tool-transitions|tool transitions]] feel like continuous motion instead of a page change — you can only animate between two states of a thing that stays on screen.

For that to work, the app needs one place that:

- holds the raw text the user typed,
- holds the *current tool*,
- knows the current viewport size (to decide the [[components/layout|layout]]), and
- owns the token data and the mapping data.

That place is `+page.svelte`. It sets up four **contexts** — shared objects any component in the tree can read without props being passed down hand to hand — once, at the root:

<!-- src/routes/+page.svelte -->

| Context | Owns | Read via |
|---|---|---|
| `ToolContext` | the current tool (`text` / `link` / `line` / `view`) | `getToolContext` |
| `BreakpointContext` | the current viewport breakpoint | `getBreakpointContext` |
| the [[data-and-tokenization/token-store\|token store]] | the source/target token arrays | `getTokenStoreContext` |
| `Alignment` | the mappings, the active mapping, click handling | `getAlignmentContext` |

The order matters. `Alignment`'s constructor *takes* the token store, so the store has to exist first. This ordering is also what guarantees there is exactly **one** owner of the token arrays: `QuoteWorkbench` and `Alignment` both read the same store rather than each keeping a copy. See [[data-and-tokenization/token-store]] for why two copies used to be a recurring source of bugs.

Beyond the contexts, `+page.svelte` also:

- owns the source / target / authorship text, and the open/closed state of the side panels;
- owns the **arrow launch** — the one-shot animation that commits the text and advances from Text tool into Link tool (see [[tools-and-mediums/tool-transitions#the-arrow-launch-text--link]]);
- calls `initAlignmentShortcuts(alignment)` once in `onMount`, registering the document-level keyboard shortcuts (see [[tools-and-mediums/mediums-and-keyboard-navigation#document-level-shortcuts]]).

## `QuoteWorkbench.svelte`

This is the centre column — the thing the side panels flank. Its job is to render the *right view* for the current tool, using token data it reads from the store:

- **Text tool** — two plain textareas (source, target), so the user can paste or type freely. The source textarea filters its input live, letting only Han characters and CJK punctuation through. That filter is careful not to break **IME composition** — the input method Chinese typists use, where you type pinyin and pick a character from a menu. Mid-composition the text field briefly holds Latin letters, so the filter skips those moments and re-filters once composition finishes; typing pinyin to produce a character still works.
- **Link, Line, and View tools** — the token workspace: the source and target panels rendered as a `role="grid"` of interactive tokens. These panels are [[components/interactive-text-panels|`InteractiveSourceText` / `InteractiveTargetText`]], and what is interactive inside them depends on the tool.

In every tool, an authorship textarea sits below the workbench (locked in View tool).

One subtlety worth stating plainly: the workbench does **not** push tokens into `Alignment`. The single thing it pushes is the raw text, via `alignment.setMeta({ sourceText, targetText, authorship })` inside an `$effect` (Svelte's mechanism for reacting to changes). `Alignment` then derives its own token view from the shared store. So the store stays the one owner, and there is no two-way sync to keep honest.

### Tokens stay continuous across edits

When the user splits or merges a line in Line tool, the token arrays are re-derived, some tokens move to different lines, and the panel that changed grows or shrinks — which shifts everything below it. Rather than let that land as an abrupt layout jump, the app **animates** the rearrangement: the tokens that move slide to their new positions, and the panel heights tween smoothly.

`QuoteWorkbench` is what makes that animation possible. It tags each panel wrapper with the DOM references the animation needs — bundled into an `editScope()` that includes both panel wrappers, their scroll boxes, *and* the authorship field — and forwards split/merge requests into the [[data-and-tokenization/token-store|token store]], which owns and runs the animation itself. The full mechanism, and why it is one coordinated motion rather than several independent ones, is in [[data-and-tokenization/token-store#the-line-edit-animation-splitmerge]].

### One navigator for both interactive tools

The Link tool and Line tool both need keyboard navigation between the elements in the token grid — but they navigate *different* things (tokens vs. split/merge controls). Rather than build two navigators, `QuoteWorkbench` creates a single one and reconfigures it per tool. See [[tools-and-mediums/mediums-and-keyboard-navigation]].

